<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results on Discriminative Task</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we evaluate our method on discriminative tasks using AMBER (discriminative VQA) and POPE (existence VQA) datasets,
with results shown in Tab. <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Results on Open-ended Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Mitigating Multimodal Hallucinations via Gradient-based Self-Reflection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Tab. <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.2 Results on Discriminative Task ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Mitigating Multimodal Hallucinations via Gradient-based Self-Reflection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
The findings confirm that our method achieves a better balance between precision and recall, resulting in consistently higher F1 scores and improved accuracy.
Notably, for the high-performance baseline Intern-VL2, other methods degrade its original performance, whereas ours effectively preserves it, likely due to its bias awareness.
Additionally, we observe variation in performance improvements across different question categories and baseline MLLMs.</p>
</div>
